Chapter 8
Make or buy
التصنيع أو الشراء
Orontes makes the metal lids for its tahini jars in a small pressing shop at one end of the factory. A supplier offers to deliver the same lids for 42 cents each. The costing system says a lid costs 48 cents to make, so buying looks like an obvious saving of six cents on three and a half million lids.
It is not obvious, and on the figures in this chapter it is wrong. A make-or-buy decision is the relevant-costing apparatus of Chapters 5 and 6 applied to a particular question, and the answer turns on which of the 48 cents would actually stop being spent — which is never all of them and is sometimes less than half.
Figure F708-01 is your map.
Figure F708-01. Chapter 8 roadmap: what making really costs, what buying releases, and what the numbers cannot say.
