WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 5 separated avoidable from unavoidable fixed costs and warned that an allocated share of a common cost is almost never avoidable. Chapter 6 added that a released resource has a value only where an alternative use exists. Those two sentences decide every make-or-buy question in this chapter.
8.1  What the costing system says, and what it means
Figure F708-02 is the lid, as the costing system reports it.
Figure F708-02. The cost of a lid, as reported
@table
Element | Cost a lid | Annual cost on 3,500,000 lids | Behaviour
Direct materials | 0.18 | 630,000 | Variable
Direct labour | 0.10 | 350,000 | Variable
Variable overhead | 0.06 | 210,000 | Variable
Fixed overhead absorbed | 0.14 | 490,000 | Fixed
Total | 0.48 | 1,680,000 |
@end
The first three lines stop if the lids are bought. The fourth is the question. Absorbed fixed overhead of $490,000 is a share of the factory's fixed costs allocated to the pressing shop by whatever basis the system uses, and the amount that would actually stop is a matter of fact rather than of allocation.
Suppose the facts are these: the pressing shop has its own supervisor, whose $175,000 post would be eliminated, and the remaining $315,000 is the shop's share of factory rent, insurance, heating and head office costs, all of which continue whether the shop presses lids or stands empty.
Then the relevant cost of making is the three variable lines plus the supervisor: 0.18 + 0.10 + 0.06 = 0.34 a lid, which is 3,500,000 × 0.34 = 1,190,000, plus the avoidable $175,000, giving 1,190,000 + 175,000 = 1,365,000. Per lid that is 1,365,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 0.39.
The supplier wants 0.42. Buying costs 3,500,000 × 0.42 = 1,470,000 against a relevant cost of making of $1,365,000, so buying would make the company 1,470,000 − 1,365,000 = 105,000 worse off a year. Orontes should go on making its lids.
EXAM TRAP   The three ways this goes wrong
Comparing the supplier's price with the full cost of 0.48. That treats $315,000 of unavoidable overhead as a saving, and it is the error the question is built to catch.
Comparing with the variable cost alone, at 0.34. That ignores the supervisor's post, which genuinely does disappear, and understates the cost of making.
Forgetting that the unavoidable $315,000 does not vanish: if the lids are bought, that overhead is re-absorbed by the other products, whose reported costs rise. Nothing has been saved and several products now look worse.
