re-absorbed | يُعاد تحميلها | réimputé
pressing shop | ورشة الكبس | atelier de presse
basis of allocation | أساس التوزيع | base de répartition
@end
SECTION CHECK   Test yourself
SC8-1  In a make-or-buy decision, absorbed fixed overhead that continues after the change is:
A.	irrelevant.
B.	relevant, because it is a cost of making.
C.	added to the supplier's price.
D.	avoidable.
SC8-2  The relevant cost of making, on 3,500,000 lids with variable costs of 0.34 and avoidable fixed costs of 175,000, is:
A.	1,365,000
B.	1,190,000
C.	1,680,000
D.	1,470,000
SC8-3  At a supplier price of 0.42, buying 3,500,000 lids would:
A.	cost 105,000 a year more than making them.
B.	save 210,000 a year.
C.	save 105,000 a year.
D.	make no difference.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
8.2  What buying releases
The arithmetic above assumes the pressing shop would simply stand empty. If it would not, the comparison changes, and the change is an opportunity cost of the kind Chapter 6 defined.
Suppose the floor space and the two presses, once free, could be used to make a new line of sesame bars earning a contribution of $200,000 a year. That $200,000 is receivable only if the lids are bought, so it belongs on the buying side.
Buying now costs 1,470,000 − 200,000 = 1,270,000 in net terms, against $1,365,000 for making, so buying is better by 1,365,000 − 1,270,000 = 95,000 a year. The recommendation reverses on a figure that appears nowhere in the costing system.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the indifference price
Step 1. The question. At what supplier price would Orontes be indifferent, assuming the space stands empty?
Step 2. The arithmetic. Making costs $1,365,000 a year, so the company is indifferent at a price of 1,365,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 0.39 a lid.
Step 3. With the alternative use. If the released space earns $200,000, the company can afford to pay 1,365,000 + 200,000 = 1,565,000, which is 1,565,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 0.447 a lid.
Step 4. Reading it. The supplier's 0.42 sits between the two. Whether to buy depends entirely on whether the alternative use is real, and the negotiation with the supplier should be conducted knowing that 0.447 is the ceiling and 0.39 is the walk-away point.
Check. The indifference price is the most useful single output of a make-or-buy analysis, because it converts a yes-or-no answer into a negotiating position. A buyer who knows the ceiling can ask for a better price; one who knows only that 0.42 beats 0.48 will accept whatever is offered below 0.48.
Figure F708-03 sets the cases out.
Figure F708-03. Make or buy, three cases
@table
Case | Cost of making | Cost of buying | Better by
Space stands empty | 1,365,000 | 1,470,000 | Making, by 105,000
Space earns 200,000 | 1,365,000 | 1,270,000 | Buying, by 95,000
Space earns 200,000 and the supplier charges 0.39 | 1,365,000 | 1,165,000 | Buying, by 200,000
@end
Two further elements belong in the figures whenever a question supplies them.
One-off costs of switching. Redundancy payments, the loss on selling the presses, the cost of qualifying the supplier, the write-off of specialised tooling. These fall on the buying side in the first year and nowhere thereafter, which is why a make-or-buy decision should be evaluated over several years and discounted, as Chapter 16 does.
Changes in other costs. Buying may raise inward freight, inspection and inventory; it may lower scrap, rework and insurance. Each belongs in the comparison if it changes.
