8.3  The qualitative factors
The outcome names these explicitly, and in practice they decide more make-or-buy questions than the arithmetic does.
Quality. A supplier's lid is not necessarily the company's lid. Where the component affects the customer's experience — a seal that leaks, a cap that cross-threads — the cost of a quality failure is far larger than six cents a unit and falls on the brand rather than on the component.
Dependability of supply. A lid that arrives late stops a filling line. The questions are whether the supplier can be relied on, whether there is a second source, how far away it is, and what happens in a strike, a storm or a border closure. Book 2's chapter on just-in-time made the same point from the inventory side: removing a buffer transfers risk to the supplier's reliability.
Control over the process and the schedule. Making keeps the ability to change a specification quickly, to run a short batch for a trial, and to prioritise one product over another. Buying gives that up to somebody else's production plan.
Confidentiality and intellectual property. A supplier making a component to a specification learns the specification. Where the component embodies something the company regards as its own, outsourcing is a transfer of knowledge as well as of work.
The supplier's future conduct. A supplier that wins the work gains leverage: the company has sold its presses, dismissed its operators, and cannot easily go back. The price offered in the first year is not always the price in the fifth, and the standard protection is a contract of several years with a price mechanism rather than a single-year quotation.
Core competence. A company should be slow to outsource what it is distinctively good at and quicker to outsource what anybody can do. Pressing lids is not what Orontes is for; roasting and grinding sesame is.
The workforce and the community. Closing a shop means redundancies, which cost money, morale and reputation, and in some jurisdictions require consultation that takes months.
Figure F708-04 sets the two sides out.
Figure F708-04. The qualitative case, both ways
@table
Factor | Argues for making | Argues for buying
Quality | The company controls it | A specialist may do it better
Dependability | No third party to fail | A supplier with several plants may be more robust
Control of schedule | Full control | The supplier's plan governs
Confidentiality | Nothing leaves the company | Specifications must be shared
Capacity | Uses space the company owns | Releases space for better uses
Expertise | Keeps skills in house | Buys skills the company lacks
Cost behaviour | Fixed costs are already incurred | Converts fixed costs into variable ones
@end
The last row deserves its own sentence, because it connects this chapter to Chapter 4. Buying converts a fixed cost into a variable one: the company pays only for the lids it needs, so its breakeven falls and its operating leverage falls with it. For a company whose demand is uncertain that is worth something real, and it is an argument for buying that has nothing to do with the price per lid.
