8.4  Variants of the same question
The outcome's phrase "make or buy" covers a family of decisions with identical arithmetic, and a question may use any of them.
Outsourcing a service. Payroll, catering, cleaning, information technology, internal audit. The analysis is the same: what stops being spent, what is released, what is given up.
In-sourcing. The reverse decision, where a company presently buying decides to make. Here the fixed costs are the ones being added rather than avoided, and the comparison is between the supplier's price and the incremental cost of making, including the new fixed costs and the capital required — which turns it into the capital investment decision of Chapter 14.
Buying a part of the volume. Often the best answer is neither all nor nothing: make the base load in house, where the fixed costs are already committed, and buy the peak from a supplier, where the alternative is overtime or a second shift. The arithmetic ranks the two sources at the margin, exactly as Chapter 7 did.
Closing one plant of several. The same table, with the avoidable costs of one site on one side and the extra transport and overtime at the others on the opposite side.
Figure F708-05 shows the base-load variant, which the exam likes because it needs two calculations.
Figure F708-05. Make the base, buy the peak
@table
Volume band | Cost of making a unit | Cost of buying a unit | Source
First 3,000,000 lids | 0.34 variable, fixed already committed | 0.42 | Make
Next 500,000, within capacity | 0.34 | 0.42 | Make
Beyond capacity, needing overtime | 0.34 plus an overtime premium of 0.11 | 0.42 | Buy, if the premium exceeds 0.08
@end
