Chapter summary
Figure F708-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F708-07. Chapter 8 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A lid reported at 0.48 is not a lid costing 0.48 to make: the relevant cost is the variable 0.18 + 0.10 + 0.06 = 0.34 plus the fixed costs that genuinely stop, which here is a $175,000 supervisor and not the $315,000 of factory and head office overhead that continues. So making costs 3,500,000 × 0.34 = 1,190,000 plus 175,000, or 1,365,000, against 3,500,000 × 0.42 = 1,470,000 to buy — and Orontes should keep making, by $105,000 a year. Released capacity changes that: a pressing shop that could earn $200,000 of contribution makes buying better by 1,365,000 − 1,270,000 = 95,000, and the recommendation turns on a figure the costing system does not contain. The indifference price of 1,365,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 0.39 is the most useful output, because it is a negotiating position rather than a verdict. The qualitative factors — quality, dependability, control, confidentiality, the supplier's future leverage, core competence and the workforce — decide many of these cases outright, and buying also converts fixed cost into variable, which lowers the breakeven and the operating leverage. And the same arithmetic serves outsourcing a service, in-sourcing a part, buying only the peak volume and closing one plant of several.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P8-01  In a make-or-buy decision, the relevant cost of making includes:
A.	variable costs plus avoidable fixed costs.
B.	the full absorbed cost.
C.	variable costs only.
D.	allocated head office costs.
P8-02  Fixed overhead that continues whichever option is chosen is:
A.	excluded from the comparison.
B.	added to the cost of buying.
C.	added to the cost of making.
D.	halved between the two.
P8-03  Variable costs of 0.34 on 3,500,000 units with avoidable fixed costs of 175,000 give a relevant cost of making of:
A.	1,365,000
B.	1,190,000
C.	1,680,000
D.	175,000
P8-04  Buying at 0.42 a unit for 3,500,000 units costs:
A.	1,470,000
B.	1,365,000
C.	1,190,000
D.	105,000
P8-05  Released capacity that could earn 200,000 of contribution:
A.	reduces the net cost of buying by 200,000.
B.	increases the cost of buying.
C.	is irrelevant.
D.	is added to the cost of making.
P8-06  The indifference price on a relevant making cost of 1,365,000 for 3,500,000 units is:
A.	0.39
B.	0.42
C.	0.48
D.	0.34
P8-07  Which is a qualitative factor?
A.	The risk that the supplier fails to deliver on time
B.	The variable cost of making
C.	The supplier's quoted price
D.	The avoidable fixed cost
P8-08  Buying rather than making usually:
A.	lowers the breakeven point by converting fixed cost into variable.
B.	raises the breakeven point.
C.	raises operating leverage.
D.	leaves the cost structure unchanged.
P8-09  Making the base load and buying the peak is sensible when:
A.	the marginal cost of making beyond capacity exceeds the supplier's price.
B.	the supplier is always cheaper.
C.	the company has unlimited capacity.
D.	fixed costs are avoidable.
P8-10  In-sourcing a bought component is evaluated as:
A.	a capital investment, because capacity and capital must be added.
B.	a pricing decision.
C.	a working capital decision.
D.	a tax decision.
Case-style practice: the lid contract
The supplier returns with a firm offer: 0.40 a lid for three years, with a price linked to the metal index thereafter, on condition that Orontes closes the pressing shop. Orontes's relevant cost of making is $1,365,000 a year on 3,500,000 lids. Closing would cost $120,000 in redundancy, and the two presses would fetch $60,000 against a book value of $310,000. The shop's floor space has no identified alternative use. The supplier has one plant, 400 kilometres away.
Required. Evaluate the offer with figures over three years, ignoring discounting. State your recommendation and the single condition you would attach. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC8-1  Answer A. A cost that continues cannot differ between the options.
B is wrong. It is a cost of the factory, not of the decision.
C is wrong. Adding it to the supplier's price flatters making.
D is wrong. Continuing is the opposite of avoidable.
SC8-2  Answer A. 1,190,000 + 175,000 = 1,365,000.
B is wrong. That omits the avoidable fixed costs.
C is wrong. That is the full absorbed cost.
D is wrong. That is the cost of buying.
SC8-3  Answer A. 1,470,000 − 1,365,000 = 105,000 worse off.
B is wrong. Nothing of that size is saved.
C is wrong. The sign is the wrong way round.
D is wrong. The difference is material.
SC8-4  Answer A. The contribution is earned only if the lids are bought.
B is wrong. Making forgoes it, so it is not a cost of making in the table.
C is wrong. It is the figure that reverses the decision.
D is wrong. It is a benefit, not a price.
SC8-5  Answer A. The price at which the two are equal.
B is wrong. Full cost includes unavoidable overhead.
C is wrong. The quote is what is being tested.
D is wrong. Variable cost omits avoidable fixed costs.
SC8-6  Answer A. They arise only on the buying option, once.
B is wrong. They are future and differ.
C is wrong. They do not arise if making continues.
D is wrong. They are an explicit payment.
SC8-7  Answer A. Dependability is a judgment, not a figure.
B is wrong. That is a cost.
C is wrong. That is a cost.
D is wrong. That is a computed price.
SC8-8  Answer A. Paying per unit replaces a fixed commitment.
B is wrong. Fixed costs fall.
C is wrong. Leverage falls with the fixed base.
D is wrong. The structure changes materially.
SC8-9  Answer A. Core competence should stay inside.
B is wrong. Commodity work is the easiest to outsource.
C is wrong. Low-value components are usual outsourcing candidates.
D is wrong. Packaging is commonly bought in.
SC8-10  Answer A. Capacity and capital must be created.
B is wrong. Inventory is a consequence, not the decision.
C is wrong. Pricing is a separate question.
D is wrong. Tax is an adjustment, not the frame.
SC8-11  Answer A. At the margin the supplier is cheaper.
B is wrong. Full cost is not the test.
C is wrong. Then everything should be bought.
D is wrong. With unlimited capacity there is no peak problem.
SC8-12  Answer A. Saying so prevents the commonest error.
B is wrong. Full costs hide the difference.
C is wrong. A complete list is not an analysis.
D is wrong. The rate is an accounting device.
SC8-13  Answer A. Name the value at which the answer changes.
B is wrong. An assumed figure is an invented one.
C is wrong. Capacity is central.
D is wrong. The outcome asks for a recommendation.
SC8-14  Answer A. Chosen factors show judgment.
B is wrong. A recital shows memory.
C is wrong. Figures rarely settle these decisions alone.
D is wrong. The calculation frames the discussion.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Variable cost a unit | 2.80: 1.40 + 0.90 + 0.50 = 2.80.
Annual variable cost | 1,120,000: 400,000 × 2.80 = 1,120,000.
Avoidable fixed costs | 69,000: 60,000 + 9,000 = 69,000.
Total relevant cost of making | 1,189,000: 1,120,000 + 69,000 = 1,189,000.
Cost of buying | 1,280,000: 400,000 × 3.20 = 1,280,000.
Better by | Making, by 91,000: 1,280,000 − 1,189,000 = 91,000.
Indifference price | 2.9725: 1,189,000 ÷ 400,000 = 2.9725.
What it tells you | The supplier is 23 cents a unit too expensive, and the company's negotiating ceiling is about 2.97 — unless a use is found for the released capacity, in which case the ceiling rises by whatever that use earns, divided by 400,000.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P8-01  Answer A. Variable plus avoidable fixed.
B is wrong. Absorbed cost includes unavoidable overhead.
C is wrong. Avoidable fixed costs genuinely stop.
D is wrong. Head office costs continue.
P8-02  Answer A. It does not differ, so it is excluded.
B is wrong. That would flatter making.
C is wrong. That would flatter buying.
D is wrong. Splitting an irrelevant figure is still irrelevant.
