Case-style set: the lid contract
The figures over three years. Making costs 1,365,000 × 3 = 4,095,000. Buying at 0.40 costs 3,500,000 × 0.40 = 1,400,000 a year, which is 1,400,000 × 3 = 4,200,000, plus $120,000 of redundancy, less $60,000 from selling the presses: 4,200,000 + 120,000 = 4,320,000, less 60,000, giving 4,260,000. Making is cheaper by 4,260,000 − 4,095,000 = 165,000 over the three years, or about $55,000 a year.
Three figures that do not belong. The $310,000 book value of the presses is sunk; only the $60,000 they would fetch is relevant. The $315,000 a year of unavoidable overhead continues whichever is chosen. And the full cost of 0.48 a lid, which makes the offer look like an easy saving, contains both.
Recommendation: decline the offer at 0.40 and go on making. Tell the supplier that the walk-away price is 0.39 before switching costs and about 0.38 once the redundancy and the loss on the presses are spread over three years, and invite a revised quotation.
The condition I would attach to any future acceptance. A second source, or a contractual commitment that makes one unnecessary. The supplier has one plant 400 kilometres away, and a lid that does not arrive stops a filling line making 3,500,000 jars a year at a contribution of $4.80 each. One week of lost production is roughly 67,000 jars and $320,000 of contribution — six years of the saving the offer promises. Before closing a shop the company owns, I would want either a second supplier qualified or a stock of lids large enough to cover the longest plausible interruption, and the cost of that stock put into the comparison.
